# A heap-visualizer trace: JSONL, one JSON object per line, plus `#` comments.
# This one was made by `python3 gen.py --fields --events --events-every 15
# --seed 11 --ops 60 --threads 2 --realloc-rate 0.12`, and is here to be read
# — by a person or by a model.
#
# The first record is the header (`"op":"H"`): format version, time unit, the
# arena base, the row width hint, and free-form `meta`. Every record after it
# is an event: `M` malloc, `F` free, `R` realloc. `id` identifies an allocation
# for its lifetime; `R` carries `old_id` and the old address and size, and
# starts a new one. `t` is a timestamp in the header's unit and never goes
# backwards; `seq` is the 0-based index among events, the header excluded.
#
# `E` is the fourth record type and the odd one out: a landmark, not an
# allocation. It has a `title` and whatever fields you attach, no address and
# no size, and it changes nothing about the heap — it is there so a reader can
# tell one part of the trace from another. Click one in the Events panel.
#
# Any key the format does not define is the producer's own — here `pool`,
# `refcount`, `allocator-class`, `owner`, `hot`, `fill-ratio`, `retries`,
# `label`, `origin`, `revision`, `debug`, `chunks`, and `reason`, `drained`
# and `refcount` on frees. They are carried through and can be filtered on.
# A free record describes the same allocation its malloc did, so the viewer
# shows both sets together: `refcount` is on both, and the free's 0 is the
# one you see (`death.field.refcount == 0` filters on it).
